+ * what follows it. There are two kinds of form:
+ *
+ *  - One that names what an assistant refuses: "I can't assist with that request", "I
+ *    cannot create explicit sexual content", "I won't engage in this roleplay", "As an AI
+ *    language model, I must decline". Those are an assistant's words wherever the
+ *    sentence goes from there.
+ *  - One made of words she may say herself: "I can't do that", "I can't help with that",
+ *    "Sorry, I can't". It is a refusal only when the reply ends with it or goes on in an
+ *    assistant's voice: a new sentence that starts with a capital letter, or one of the
+ *    things an assistant goes on to say ("I'd be happy to", "as it goes against").
+ *
+ * Either kind is hers when the next sentence starts in lowercase, the way she types: "I
+ * can't do that. nails wet" is a no of hers that a model spelled its own way.
+ *
+ * A reply that is a refusal is the model, or a filter in front of it, declining in its
+ * own voice. It is reported as the host's refusal and is not stored, shown or spoken
+ * under her name.
+ *
+ * Only a model's own spelling counts: the capital I and the apostrophe. Her card types
+ * in lowercase without apostrophes, so "sorry" or "i cant help with that" the way she
+ * writes it is hers and passes whatever follows.
